  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Emergency livelihood support to internally displaced persons affected by camp closures and host community members for the development of an inclusive market system in Iraq</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Following the conflict with ISIL between 2014 and 2017, Iraq experienced mass displacement, with nearly six million people becoming internally displaced. Although a significant portion had returned home by August 2024, about 1.1 million remained displaced amidst a fragile economy and limited employment opportunities, especially for women. Camp closures have compounded hardships, including loss of livelihoods and increased displacement risks.  To address these challenges, a proposed UNIDO project aims to promote entrepreneurship among unemployed IDPs and residents in areas of return or resettlement by providing development programs, essential equipment, and tools. The project encourages the formation of producer groups to enhance market access. It aligns with the Humanitarian-Development-Peace Nexus approach, aiming for socioeconomic stability and peace through private sector development. It adopts the Market System Development approach to address systemic market constraints, fostering inclusive and sustainable economic growth and improved livelihoods. Project locations and sectors will be decided in consultation with authorities and based on IDP needs and the severity of their situations as the camp closures, return, resettlement, and local integration is still an ongoing process.  Note: The project manager is</narrative>
  </description>
